# Survey QA Tool

A Python CLI tool that compares a Decipher XML survey against a client questionnaire (Word/PDF) and outputs a structured QA report. The questionnaire is always the source of truth.

## Module structure

The package is split into focused sub-modules with a strict dependency direction — lower layers never import from higher ones:

```
survey_qa/
├── core/          ← models + utils only. No I/O, no business logic.
├── xml_parser/    ← Decipher XML → SurveyModel. Imports core only.
├── doc_parser/    ← docx/pdf + LLM → QuestionnaireModel. Imports core only.
├── checks/        ← QA check registry + rules. Imports core only.
└── api/           ← FastAPI + CLI. The ONLY module that imports from all others.
```

Dependency direction: `core` → (`xml_parser` | `doc_parser` | `checks`) → `api`

## Architecture data flow

Two independent parsers converge on the same Pydantic models, which the check layer consumes. Parsers and checks are fully decoupled.

```
Questionnaire (docx/pdf) → [doc_parser: Extractor → Chunker → LLM Parser] ─┐
                                                                              ├→ checks → Findings → api (FastAPI / CLI)
Decipher XML             → [xml_parser]                                     ─┘
```

## Questionnaire parsing is two-stage

**Stage 1 (local, free):** `python-docx` / `pdfplumber` extract raw text with structural signals (bold, indentation, blank lines, table cells). A heuristic chunker groups text into question-candidate blocks. No interpretation here — just noise reduction.

**Stage 2 (LLM, cached):** Batches of ~8 blocks go to the LLM via `instructor` + `litellm`. The response is typed as `list[ParsedQuestion]` directly — no JSON parsing. Results are cached by `sha256(block_text + model_name)` via `diskcache`. Re-running on the same doc is free.

Default model: `gpt-4o-mini`. Override with `LITELLM_MODEL` env var.

## Shared models (`src/survey_qa/core/models.py`)

All parsers output these Pydantic v2 models. Never bypass them.

- `ParsedQuestion` — label, text, type_hint, options, routing_rules, confidence, source_location
- `ParsedOption` — code, text
- `RoutingRule` — raw routing text (interpreted by routing checks)
- `Finding` — check_id, severity, question_label, message, detail

## Check registry pattern

Each check is a class in `src/survey_qa/checks/`. It must:
- Inherit from `Check` (defined in `checks/base.py`)
- Implement `run(q: ParsedQuestion, x: ParsedQuestion) -> list[Finding]`
- Self-register via the `@register_check` decorator

Adding a new check = one new class. Never modify the registry or runner to add checks.

## XML parser has no LLM

Decipher XML is deterministic. Use `lxml` only. No LLM calls in `xml_parser.py`.

## Testing rules

- Never make real LLM calls in tests. Use fixture `ParsedQuestion` objects.
- Sample `.docx`, `.pdf`, and `.xml` files live in `tests/fixtures/`.
- Mock `instructor` / `litellm` at the boundary in `llm_parser.py`.

## Key libraries

| Purpose | Library |
|---|---|
| XML parsing | `lxml` |
| docx extraction | `python-docx` |
| PDF extraction | `pdfplumber` |
| LLM structured output | `instructor` |
| LLM provider abstraction | `litellm` |
| Parse result cache | `diskcache` |
| Data models | `pydantic` v2 |
| CLI | `typer` |
| Terminal output | `rich` |
| Excel reports | `openpyxl` |
